WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:04.890
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:06.290
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:09.150
We were on The Office together, and we're best friends.

4
00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:13.270
And now we're doing the ultimate Office Lovers podcast just for you.

5
00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:17.790
Each week, we will dive deeper into the world of The Office with exclusive interviews,

6
00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:20.990
behind-the-scenes details, and lots of BFF stories.

7
00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:23.010
We're The Office Lady 6.0.

8
00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:26.920
Hey, lady.

9
00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.020
We're not in the studio together.

10
00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:30.960
We're doing a virtual record.

11
00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:38.300
Lord, we're doing this wild thing in technology where you zoom, and your face pops up in a

12
00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:39.040
little box.

13
00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:40.400
Oh, I wanted to tell you something.

14
00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:40.880
What?

15
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:47.140
I was so excited by this, and I cannot believe I did not share it with you immediately.

16
00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.520
Lady, we were in the San Francisco airport a while back.

17
00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:50.820
Okay.

18
00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:54.640
And there's a freaking old tech hole, like, museum.

19
00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.340
I mean, I'm sure it's just an exhibit.

20
00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:58.640
It was all old telephones, lady.

21
00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.260
It was called Give Me a Ring, a Telephone Retrospective.

22
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.620
I mean, I almost was, like, late for the flight.

23
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:06.720
Josh was like, where'd you go?

24
00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:07.840
I thought you were just going to the bathroom.

25
00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.580
I'm like, there's a telephone retrospective.

26
00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.020
Look, I can put these in stories.

27
00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:12.960
Can you see this?

28
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:13.900
That's crazy.

29
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:18.060
I mean, I had so many of those telephones in my own house, in my own lifetime.

30
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.040
You know, hats off to the SFO airport.

31
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:25.060
I always love it when an airport gives me something to do while I'm waiting.

32
00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:25.320
Yeah, me too.

33
00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:26.280
I appreciate it.

34
00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:26.340
Me too.

35
00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:33.220
You know, in the St. Louis airport, in the American terminal, they have this whole play space for kids.

36
00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:34.360
Oh, those are great.

37
00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.120
And my dad helped build it.

38
00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:36.720
No!

39
00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:41.960
Yes, because it's courtesy of the Magic House in St. Louis, which is a children's museum.

40
00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:45.380
And he used to work there and help build exhibits.

41
00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:50.000
And so he also built this exhibit that they gifted to the airport.

42
00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:54.620
And there's a little tower, and you can pretend like you're like an air traffic controller.

43
00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:58.740
And there's a little, you know, baggage thingy.

44
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:00.800
And you can get on a plane.

45
00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.020
Yeah, and it's all like a play space.

46
00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:05.920
And when I walked by it, I was like, God, I love that.

47
00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:10.320
And how cool is it that every time you walk past it, you can think of your dad?

48
00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:10.880
I know.

49
00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:12.040
I took a picture of it.

50
00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:13.760
I'll give it to you for stories.

51
00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:17.700
Old telephones and an airport play space in stories.

52
00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:19.320
Lady, I'm here for it.

53
00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:21.040
When an airport surprises you.

54
00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:22.000
Dot, dot, dot.

55
00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.680
Well, should we tell everybody what we're doing today?

56
00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:29.780
Because if you are a super fan of The Office, we think you're going to really like today's episode.

57
00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:30.800
Oh, my goodness.

58
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.240
You are going to love it.

59
00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:37.360
And it was inspired by a fan question about our life on set, specifically our trailers.

60
00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:41.220
So we're going to dig into some behind the scenes of our day-to-day on The Office.

61
00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:43.100
And it made me so happy.

62
00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:45.140
I went back in time on this one, lady.

63
00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:48.540
I have a folder on my laptop called Our Trailers.

64
00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:49.360
Did you know that?

65
00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:50.080
Yes.

66
00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:53.140
Because when we were putting together pictures for our book,

67
00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:58.640
we have a whole chapter in our book that kind of gives you the behind the scenes of the hair and makeup trailer

68
00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:00.600
and sort of life off the set.

69
00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:04.360
But I think we're going to dig in even deeper on today's episode.

70
00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:05.420
We are.

71
00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:10.920
I think one of the things I was so tickled by is that I have a folder of all the stuff for our book.

72
00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:12.140
It's called The Office BFFs.

73
00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:16.080
But somehow, this one for the trailer just made its way out.

74
00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.400
And it's just been hanging on my desktop for years.

75
00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:21.360
And I've never tidied it.

76
00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:23.040
I've never put it back where it belongs.

77
00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:23.840
You know why?

78
00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:26.300
Because it wanted to inspire this episode today.

79
00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:27.140
It's been waiting.

80
00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:28.480
I guess so.

81
00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:34.120
Well, let's kick things off with a chit-chat sent in from Harrison in Boston.

82
00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:36.200
Hi, Office Ladies.

83
00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.160
My name is Harrison from Boston, Massachusetts.

84
00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:39.560
I love the podcaSt.

85
00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:41.440
Thank you guys so much for everything that you do.

86
00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:44.100
My chit-chat question is about teachers.

87
00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:47.260
You both have talked about teachers before in the podcaSt.

88
00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.260
And Jenna, I know that your sister is a teacher.

89
00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:54.380
Could you guys share or talk about a teacher that you had who changed your life

90
00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:56.540
or who made a lifelong impact on you?

91
00:03:56.540 --> 00:04:00.860
You know, for me, it was my English teacher, Mrs. Roney, who fostered such a love of reading

92
00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:04.420
and writing and whose lessons still come back to me all these years later.

93
00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:08.680
So I was hoping that you guys could share about teachers that you have had in your lives that

94
00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:12.460
really, you know, left an impact on you or changed you for the better.

95
00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:15.060
I think teachers are so overlooked and so important.

96
00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:18.600
And I'd love to hear about the ones that really made you who you are.

97
00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:19.820
Thank you so much.

98
00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:22.640
Thanks for your question, Harrison.

99
00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:23.780
I love this question.

100
00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:27.700
Yes, my sister is currently a teacher and my mom was a teacher.

101
00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:28.440
She's retired.

102
00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:29.880
Yeah, Harrison.

103
00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:32.320
I mean, my sister Janet is a teacher.

104
00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:33.660
Oh my gosh.

105
00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:38.820
I'm going to get the number wrong, but she's been teaching pre-K for like 27 years.

106
00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:40.880
And she listens to the podcaSt.

107
00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.880
So if I got that wrong, she's going to let me know about it.

108
00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:44.980
But we love our teachers so much.

109
00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:45.960
And you're so right.

110
00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:47.880
They forever shape your life.

111
00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:54.160
Having that one teacher, and we all have that one, you know, that literally made you see

112
00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:57.400
the world in a different way or made you feel safe or made you feel seen.

113
00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:00.840
And I knew right away, two teachers popped into my mind immediately.

114
00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:01.800
Jenna, what about you?

115
00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:02.300
Same.

116
00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:04.880
I have a teacher from high school and a teacher from college.

117
00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:06.860
Okay, you go firSt.

118
00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:07.400
All right.

119
00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:08.580
Well, I'll start in high school.

120
00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:11.820
My teacher, Mrs. Marge Mineas.

121
00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:13.680
What a name.

122
00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:15.080
Yeah, Mrs. Mineas.

123
00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:18.280
She taught religion at my high school.

124
00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:22.080
I went to a Catholic girls high school in St. Louis, Missouri called Narex Hall.

125
00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:30.660
And during my time there, I did really grapple with my feelings about the Catholic faith.

126
00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:39.220
I was really troubled by certain aspects of the institution, you know?

127
00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:44.540
And I don't need to get into it here, but she was my religion teacher.

128
00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:52.500
And when I went to her, the way that she just took me so seriously and took my thoughts and

129
00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:55.900
concerns so seriously, she really counseled me.

130
00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:59.400
She spent a lot of time asking me questions.

131
00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:06.380
And then when I had to write papers in class, she let me wrestle with these topics.

132
00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:08.040
And I got A's.

133
00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:09.280
She gave me A's.

134
00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:14.080
And she didn't need me to just spit back what I was being taught.

135
00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:14.900
Do you know what I mean?

136
00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:16.280
Like, I didn't have to be fake.

137
00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:17.520
I didn't have to pretend.

138
00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:18.580
Yeah.

139
00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:24.880
She encouraged you to have your critical thinking and to really be able to explore all of your

140
00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:25.740
feelings on the subject.

141
00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:27.740
It didn't matter what it aligned with or not.

142
00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:28.280
Yes.

143
00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:32.800
And the thing that I really credit her with is that I think, especially at that time in

144
00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:35.220
my life, it was a critical point.

145
00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:40.280
And to have someone take me so seriously, it meant that I didn't abandon faith completely.

146
00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:45.440
You know, I am not a practicing Catholic today, and I still have my connection to God and to

147
00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:45.820
prayer.

148
00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:52.680
And I really credit her with that because she helped me find a way to keep that peace alive

149
00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:53.220
in me.

150
00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:58.060
I didn't have to throw everything out the door just because I didn't believe in the

151
00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:00.580
particular religion that I was being asked to practice.

152
00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:01.780
Yeah.

153
00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:03.840
That is very powerful.

154
00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:05.080
That is a real gift.

155
00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:06.940
And she was like that with all of us.

156
00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:07.880
She really cared.

157
00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:10.720
She didn't treat us like, you know, silly teenagers.

158
00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:14.520
She treated us like fully formed, interesting people.

159
00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:15.820
So I love her for that.

160
00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:17.180
What about you, Ange?

161
00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:22.300
Well, the first teacher that came to mind for me was my theater teacher in elementary

162
00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:22.700
school.

163
00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:25.580
So this was at Jakarta International School.

164
00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:30.320
And I had this teacher named Dr. Allen.

165
00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:32.700
He was an amazing theater director.

166
00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:36.660
And we would do these fantastic musicals and plays.

167
00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:42.480
But in his class, it was the first time anyone ever taught me improv.

168
00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:50.760
And I was in maybe like fourth grade and he taught us some just very like short form improv games.

169
00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:54.960
And it was like every single part of my body came alive.

170
00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:57.440
I was like, what is this?

171
00:07:57.660 --> 00:07:58.940
This is the cooleSt.

172
00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:01.120
I love it so much.

173
00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:02.920
I want to do this forever.

174
00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:09.000
It was just like electric, you know, because I had seen plays and done school plays and there

175
00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:10.880
was a script and you stuck to the script.

176
00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:19.620
But this idea of yes anding and free association and having to really listen and form a scene

177
00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:24.780
with someone else in a partnership was life-changing for me.

178
00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:32.420
And he also took us seriously and was invested and didn't think we were goofballs, but really

179
00:08:32.420 --> 00:08:34.860
wanted us to find our own comedic voice.

180
00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:36.960
And I was like, wow.

181
00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:41.420
I look back in hindsight and I'm like, how amazing to have a teacher like that in elementary.

182
00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:44.080
Yeah, that's amazing.

183
00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:48.360
Well, when I was in college, I was a theater major.

184
00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:55.320
And I never got the lead roles in anything, in the musicals, in the plays.

185
00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:59.500
I really had a lot of opportunities in like the student-led productions.

186
00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:02.820
We had this lab theater where students would kind of decide.

187
00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:05.960
And like I got to choreograph cabaret and—

188
00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:07.680
Lab theater was like some of my favorite.

189
00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:08.480
It was great.

190
00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:14.100
Like we had one student who made a musical out of schoolhouse rock cartoons.

191
00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:16.000
And I got to be in that.

192
00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:20.220
So I was always getting cast and being involved in the student lab productions, which honestly

193
00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:24.940
is really good practice for an actual professional acting career because so much of your professional

194
00:09:24.940 --> 00:09:28.440
acting career is you getting together with your buddies and making stuff.

195
00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:32.980
But in terms of like the mainstream productions, it was harder for me.

196
00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:39.980
But the tech teacher, Ron Rybkowski, he was the person where we had to take classes to

197
00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:42.800
learn how to do lights and construction and set design.

198
00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:47.060
He also got to direct one play a year in the summer.

199
00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:50.980
So if you took the summer session, he was the director of the play.

200
00:09:50.980 --> 00:09:57.440
And he gave me a lead role in my last summer session.

201
00:09:58.120 --> 00:09:59.280
What was it?

202
00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:01.020
It was Crimes of the Heart.

203
00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:02.260
Mm-hmm.

204
00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:03.860
It's a play about three sisters.

205
00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:10.720
And he cast me as the wild, chain-smoking, bad girl sister.

206
00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:12.500
It was like totally against type.

207
00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:15.120
But it was so fun.

208
00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:19.500
And it was like the confidence boost that I needed because I was going to graduate then

209
00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:20.660
after that summer session.

210
00:10:20.860 --> 00:10:23.640
And I wanted to be a professional actress and move to Los Angeles.

211
00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:29.200
And I had gone through my whole four years as a theater major never having a lead role

212
00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:29.820
in a production.

213
00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:32.380
And he finally gave me one.

214
00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:34.300
And it really was a boost of confidence.

215
00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:37.640
But beyond that, he was just always there.

216
00:10:37.780 --> 00:10:41.120
He was always in his back office if we needed anything.

217
00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:47.560
And I remember—this is such a long story—but when I went to jail and I needed someone

218
00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:49.140
to bail me out, I called him.

219
00:10:51.990 --> 00:10:57.130
It's a very long story, but I had rolled through a stop sign and I got a ticket.

220
00:10:57.570 --> 00:10:59.030
Why'd they take you to jail?

221
00:10:59.290 --> 00:11:02.510
Because I failed to appear in court for my ticket.

222
00:11:02.630 --> 00:11:02.970
Oh, no.

223
00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:03.490
I forgot.

224
00:11:03.490 --> 00:11:04.790
It was finals week.

225
00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:05.650
I just forgot.

226
00:11:06.170 --> 00:11:06.730
Oh, yeah.

227
00:11:06.730 --> 00:11:08.450
I think that happens all the time.

228
00:11:08.570 --> 00:11:08.870
I know.

229
00:11:08.990 --> 00:11:10.830
They put a warrant out for your arreSt.

230
00:11:11.330 --> 00:11:17.230
And so two police officers came and knocked on my apartment door, and they arrested me

231
00:11:17.230 --> 00:11:21.110
because I had failed to appear in court for my moving violation.

232
00:11:21.690 --> 00:11:22.970
That you forgot about.

233
00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:24.030
That I forgot about.

234
00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:25.510
And I was like, oh, my God.

235
00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:26.530
Thank you for reminding me.

236
00:11:26.570 --> 00:11:27.230
I'm so sorry.

237
00:11:27.390 --> 00:11:29.570
And they're like, yeah, we're not the reminder police.

238
00:11:29.610 --> 00:11:31.610
We're like the actual police.

239
00:11:31.730 --> 00:11:33.470
We're going to have to take you in.

240
00:11:34.030 --> 00:11:38.290
So anyway, I was there, and I was just so embarrassed.

241
00:11:38.630 --> 00:11:39.970
And I didn't know who to call.

242
00:11:39.970 --> 00:11:43.230
I needed a hundred bucks to get bailed out.

243
00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:44.430
So I called Ron.

244
00:11:44.630 --> 00:11:48.410
I called my teacher, Ron Rubkowski, and he came and he bailed me out of jail.

245
00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:52.330
And I said, I'm not going to tell my parents.

246
00:11:52.410 --> 00:11:54.410
And he said, I think you should.

247
00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:58.310
He said, it would break my heart if my daughter didn't share that with me.

248
00:11:58.890 --> 00:12:01.130
He said, I think you can share it with your parents.

249
00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:03.610
And I'm so glad he told me that because then I did.

250
00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:05.150
Do you know what I mean?

251
00:12:05.210 --> 00:12:06.410
He was just like a great man.

252
00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:09.870
He's just like a solid, solid person to have in your life.

253
00:12:09.970 --> 00:12:11.630
Great advice there for you.

254
00:12:11.830 --> 00:12:12.130
Yes.

255
00:12:12.270 --> 00:12:12.410
Yeah.

256
00:12:12.550 --> 00:12:19.250
He also took my first headshot for free so that when I went off to Los Angeles, I had

257
00:12:19.250 --> 00:12:19.610
a headshot.

258
00:12:19.710 --> 00:12:21.290
He did that for every graduating senior.

259
00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:22.330
Amazing.

260
00:12:22.790 --> 00:12:25.110
So that was a little bit of a meandering story.

261
00:12:25.350 --> 00:12:30.510
But just to say, Harrison, a big thank you to my college professor, Ron Rubkowski.

262
00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:31.630
All right.

263
00:12:31.630 --> 00:12:34.990
Well, my second teacher was my high school English teacher.

264
00:12:35.330 --> 00:12:41.290
And Harrison, I totally related to you saying that for you, it was also your English teacher.

265
00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:42.750
Carol Campbell.

266
00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:45.170
And she taught honors English.

267
00:12:45.450 --> 00:12:50.430
And she just made stories come to life.

268
00:12:50.590 --> 00:12:52.390
And I would have a lot of questions.

269
00:12:52.390 --> 00:12:58.510
And I loved digging into books and seeing if I could make connections or symbolism.

270
00:12:58.510 --> 00:13:01.110
And she was so up for the discussion.

271
00:13:01.310 --> 00:13:01.910
Do you know what I mean?

272
00:13:02.170 --> 00:13:05.770
Like she thought the way my brain worked was really interesting.

273
00:13:05.790 --> 00:13:09.010
And she encouraged that writer in me.

274
00:13:09.210 --> 00:13:13.690
My kids right now for their high school English class, Isabel and Jack are reading The Great

275
00:13:13.690 --> 00:13:18.710
Gatsby, and I read The Great Gatsby in Mrs. Campbell's English class.

276
00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:21.270
Oh, are you so excited for them to read it?

277
00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:22.590
I'm so excited.

278
00:13:22.590 --> 00:13:23.810
And they're loving it.

279
00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:28.590
And it's been really special because I just remember all the conversations I had with Mrs.

280
00:13:28.730 --> 00:13:30.730
Campbell about The Great Gatsby.

281
00:13:31.330 --> 00:13:37.990
So yeah, big love to our teachers who are just amazing and there for our kids in so many

282
00:13:37.990 --> 00:13:38.490
ways.

283
00:13:38.690 --> 00:13:40.050
Harrison, we loved your question.

284
00:13:40.290 --> 00:13:41.150
Yeah, thank you.

285
00:13:41.330 --> 00:13:43.110
Well, listen, ladies, should we take a break?

286
00:13:43.690 --> 00:13:46.270
When we come back, we're going to talk all about trailers.

287
00:13:46.710 --> 00:13:48.750
Trailers on The Office, you guys.

288
00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:53.850
And Steve Burgess, our amazing producer, shares some fun details with us.

